  5. Hadronic Top Cut Parameters:

#1    < MinPtForJets = 20. >       (GeV)                 
#2    < EtaRangeForJets = 4.5 >              
#3    < TopMass = 174.3 >          (GeV)  (direct observation (+-5.1))   
#4    < AllowedTopMassDiff = 35. > (GeV)
#5    < WMass = 80.419 >           (GeV)  (+-0.056)   
#6    < AllowedWMassDiff = 15. >   (GeV)

#7    < TopCutType = 2 >            1, 2    

 Notes:
    1) #1  - Minimal Pt (GeV) of the three jets
    2) #2  - The three jets have to be within |eta|<EtaRange
    3) #3  - Top mass
    4) #4  - The invariant mass of the three jets should be closer
             to the top mass than 35 GeV
    5) #5  - W mass
    6) #6  - The invariant mass of the two jets should be closer
             to the W mass than 15 GeV
    7) #7  - 1 -> The Hadr.Top cut is performed the way suggested in
                  the paper (from Stefano & ali). I.e. first select 3
		  jets likely from Top, then two jets out of these, likely
	 	  to come from W, and then check if the third jet is B-tagged.
		  This way gives one more cut-coloumn compare to:
             2 -> The Hadr.Top cut is performed the way suggested by Nils.
		  I.e. first seperate b-tagged and none b-tagged jets 
		  (assumed W-jets). Out of the W jets pick out all 
		  possible 2-Jet-W combinations. Then see if any of these
		  combine satisfactory with any of the b-tagged jets, to
		  make a 2W-Jets-B-Jet combination, i.e. a 3Top-Jet comb. 
  
